<p>The grown-up run continues: barbershop tales, a run of mining-camp columns from the years right after World War II, two more brushes with sudden death underground, a batch of Inez stories, and the wider circle of friends and family on Cebolla Creek. The tone stays light even where the ground underneath it isn't — a New Year's Eve dance recalled as a disaster movie, a mining fatality recounted twice from two different angles, and a difficult homecoming for Inez's mother — before closing out with more of the Cebolla ranch's cats, chores and quarrels.</p>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="twice-around-the-barber-pole">Twice Around the Barber Pole</h2>

  <p>"Do you remember the first time I cut your hair?"</p>

  <p>Our mistress posed the question as she ran the clippers around my ears. Lordy, do I remember! Every time I feel the scars on my neck and the indent in my left ear, I remember.</p>

  <p>Creede boasted three tonsorial parlors when I was a youngster. Gene Thom had a spacious shop in the Heinz building, Freddie Frank operated in the Mercantile Block and Soapy Birdsey combined barbering with a pool table and poker games under the Elks Hall. Later on, Bob Phillips and Al Hibbs opened shops on opposite sides of Main Street.</p>

  <p>Al Hibbs was a gun collector and avid hunter. It was a treat to visit his shop and get to feel the rifles that won the West as well as chew the fat about past and future hunting trips. Al collected many things.</p>

  <p>I was in his chair one day when a young fellow, who was about to leave the state, came in trying to sell a watch to other customers waiting their turn. He needed just $5.00 more for a bus ticket.</p>

  <p>The watch was well worth it and as it was passed around, Al asked, "Let me see that watch."</p>

  <p>"Jeezuscrist," he roared as he yanked his own watch from a pocket. "I'd sell this watch for $5.00 and it's a Waltham."</p>

  <p>Smitty, a driller, calmly picked two five spots from his wallet, handed one to Al and the other to the young fellow and pocketed both watches. Al had to talk like a Dutch uncle to retrieve his watch.</p>

  <p>Inez has cut my hair for over 40 years now and as near as I can remember she opened only one wound she wasn't able to close with a butterfly patch. After the first mangling with a straight razor we have foregone shaving my neck or around my ears.</p>
</section>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="miners-after-world-war-ii-a-unique-breed">Miners After World War II: A Unique Breed</h2>

  <p>Shortly after World War II, jobs were readily available in the mines of Creede where metals needed for the war effort would be equally in demand for a peacetime economy. Many hometown boys returned to underground work, and veterans from near and far drifted in to become miners.</p>

  <p>It was a wild period of readjustment for everyone and this famous old mining camp had just the tonic to help in the transition to a normal life. At least as normal as the patients desired.</p>

  <p>I was a mucker for a driller named Bob Oates, who came from a noted mining family in Creede. Bob had served in the Navy on a destroyer and experienced kamikaze attacks off Okinawa.</p>

  <p>Neil Walker, George Kershner and Bill Mathews were our muleskinners. All saw action in Army and Marine ground units. George Logue, who helped recapture two Aleutian islands, and John Dabney were driving a prospect raise called 61 and near them Tommy Thompson and Ruben Wesander, veterans both, were mining in 63 raise.</p>

  <p>Frankie Davis and Paul Murray were leasing a block of ground on the 850 level. Red Johnson and Shorty Dunkel were stoping on 6 level off the Albion cross-cut. Freddie Birdsey and Chappie LeZotte were tramming and hooking buckets for George Hatfield and James "Zero" Ott.</p>

  <p>In 42 raise, Lee Skinner and his son Gavin were mining a rich silver vein above 5 level. Lee always wrapped his false teeth in a napkin and placed them in his lunch bucket before setting up his drill. Woodrow Weaver, a brother-in-law, unwrapped Lee's teeth one day, placed a horse turd between them and returned the choppers to his lunch bucket. The roar could have been heard downtown when Lee sat down to eat.</p>

  <p>Paul Weaver, Ed McCargish and a Swede from Alamosa, Charley Johnson, were our hoistmen. Erick Nelson, I.D. Crawford and Glen Archer were our supervisors.</p>

  <p>There was an element of danger in every move we made underground. The hairiest part of the day was being lowered to different levels with our feet on the rims of sump buckets and our hands holding tightly to the small cable spooling from air hoists.</p>

  <p>On one such trip, Gavin Skinner and I survived the fastest free-spooling drop of three levels. We knew we were goners when we zipped past 6 level with no hint of braking. The flame from Gavin's carbide lamp pointed straight up and his hard hat was floating above his head. The hoistman managed to stop us a few feet from the floor where we yo-yoed several seconds on a stretched cable that had to be replaced.</p>

  <p>We lost a few during this period and were lucky we didn't lose more from the mining methods and conditions in the "hole".</p>

  <p>A premature blast claimed Ruben and severely injured Tommy Thompson. Clarence Martin, who had been captured by the Japanese on Bataan and endured hell as a prisoner in Japan, died in 60 raise from a long fall. Clarence told me all he dreamed about in prison camp was vegetables. During the time we worked together, his lunch consisted of a tomato, slice of cabbage, carrots and raw potatoes.</p>

  <p>We rode to and from work in pickup trucks fitted with benches and covered with homemade plywood boxes that were much appreciated in 40-degree-below-zero weather. We jumped, on the ride home, when they slowed at the turn above Tomkins Hardware and slid partway to the bars, where we recovered from the tensions of the day with grog and boasting.</p>

  <p>There were three full-time drinking establishments, and we tried to keep all economically sound.</p>

  <p>Tucker's Bar and Grill wasn't our favorite but provided the most excitement. The great white ape was a female, or so we believed, who looked like she had been trampled by a herd of buffalo and drug around by coyotes. She had an extraordinary disposition that turned violent with a passing cloud.</p>

  <p>We were lined up on the stools one evening when she appeared near the end of the bar with an automatic pistol. Suffering a hangover, she had decided to kill her husband and anyone else within range. The first shot from the wildly gyrating barrel shattered a window, the next slug parted the hair of a lady patron. By that time, Woody, Gavin and I were trying to squeeze three bodies under one small table. Her husband had presence of mind enough to count the reports.</p>

  <p>He knew how many shells were in the gun and, at the sound of the first click, he vaulted the bar, grabbed the pistol and dumped her on her posterior with a solid right hand. Another night, we were being hassled by a mooching ex-sailor who claimed to have had two ships shot out from under his feet. Usually we put up with his baloney and popped for a drink, but this night we were having a serious discussion about females so I told him to go play with his boats in the bathtub. He disappeared for several minutes and returned with a high-powered rifle which he aimed right at my chest and offered me time to say a prayer before he blew me to hell. Woody stepped between us, took the rifle with one hand and put the sailor to sleep with the other before I could even start "My Father Who Art In Heaven."</p>

  <p>It was a consortium of hardworking, rough living characters who produced much and complained little. I remember a little fellow I knew only as "Hap" who could whistle like a mockingbird and entertained us while Peggy accompanied on the piano. Hank and Ray, lifelong friends, could always leave us laughing. I remember Tank and Ernie who jumped into battle in Europe with the 82nd and Screaming Eagles divisions and I remember Billy and Kayo, Earl J. and Robert.</p>

  <p>To all of them and more who feared "The Great Ape" and worked at the Amethyst in the late '40s, we offer this:</p>

  <div class="source-doc-poem">
    <p>
      Migawd pard, that rock was hard but we broke her deep and clean,<br>
      The shifter's sass, the dust, the gas, the worst I've ever seen<br>
      We'll wash it down when we hit town in Tucker's Bar and Grill.<br>
      They'll think the smell is straight from hell when we come off the hill.<br>
      The driver slows, looks down his nose as we scramble with our gear<br>
      And form a line to a neon sign that flashes "Cold Draft Beer".<br>
      Pug's at the bar and Tuck's not far, as we shuffle through the door<br>
      To take the stools of other fools passed out around the floor.<br>
      The great white ape with eyes agape has staggered from the rear.<br>
      A parrot's screech stops Bernie's speech, its cage beside a mirror.<br>
      A dog gone mad bursts from a pad, a monkey on its back,<br>
      The white ape's pet, his passions met, leaps up and scratches Jack.<br>
      Our ex-Marine sounds gruff and mean, the jungle sounds remind<br>
      He learned to kill on Bougainville, it still torments his mind.<br>
      We yell to Pete, "Your time to treat." He orders, "Pour a round,<br>
      A toast to you and the filthiest crew the devil ever found,"<br>
      A shot and beer for our engineer, old Paul has come in late.<br>
